#prediction(title: [Prospective Cohort: Do Mild / Lifetime-Energy Individuals Transition to Moderate or Severe ME/CFS?])[
The single largest evidence gap in the mild/lifetime-energy topic is whether constitutional low-energy individuals later cross into a ME/CFS state requiring PEM. A prospective cohort would test this directly @Collin2015childFatigue @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue.

*Prediction.* Among individuals with a documented lifelong low-energy constitution (low baseline energy, no PEM at baseline), a subset will, after a triggering stressor (infection, major life stress, overactivity), develop a *new, definite* post-exertional malaise and functional decline — i.e., acquire ME/CFS — at a higher rate than matched peers with normal baseline energy. The transition would be marked by the *emergence of PEM*, not by a change in resting energy level alone @Sunnquist2015lifelongFatigue.

*Falsification.* If lifetime-low-energy individuals develop ME/CFS at no higher rate than normal-baseline peers, or if their later functional decline is explained entirely by cumulative overactivity (the high-energy premorbid route @Harvey2009neurasthenia) rather than baseline reserve, then the low-reserve-ceiling predisposing model is unsupported and constitutional tiredness is not a predisposing trajectory.

*Measurement.* Screen a low-energy and a normal-baseline cohort on baseline energy level and PEM presence; follow prospectively for emergence of PEM (2-day CPET where feasible), functional capacity, and severity transitions. Severity transitions must be measured, not self-reported @Brown2013EnergyEnvelope.

*(Severity applicability: mild → moderate/severe transition.)*

*Consequence:* This one study would determine whether lifelong low-energy patients are a predisposed population needing early PEM screening and pacing, or simply the visible tail of normal tiredness — resolving the central question of this integration.

*(Origin: brainstorm — R2.1.)*
